# upnp-blacklist.json — despite the extension, this is parsed line by line, NOT as JSON.
#
# Copy this file to upnp-blacklist.json to activate it. It is optional and is re-read on
# every use, so edits apply without a restart.
#
# One rule per line. A rule can match on any combination of internal client
# IP, external port, and internal port — a field you leave out acts as a
# wildcard. A rule blocks a mapping only when every field it DOES specify
# matches the request. Port fields accept a single port or a START-END range
# (inclusive on both ends).
#
# Shorthand forms:
#   PORT             blocks that external port for every internal client
#   PORT-PORT        blocks that external port range for every internal client
#   IP:PORT          blocks that external port only for that internal client IP
#   IP:PORT-PORT     blocks that external port range only for that IP
#
# Full form (any subset of ip / ext / int, any order, comma-separated,
# ext/int accept ranges the same way):
#   ip=IP                     blocks every mapping for that IP, any ports
#   ext=PORT or ext=LOW-HIGH  same as the bare PORT / PORT-PORT shorthand
#   int=PORT or int=LOW-HIGH  blocks that internal port/range, any IP, any ext
#   ip=IP,ext=PORT            same as the IP:PORT shorthand
#   ip=IP,int=PORT            blocks that internal port only for that IP
#   ext=PORT,int=PORT         blocks that specific ext+int pairing, any IP
#   ip=IP,ext=PORT,int=PORT   blocks only that exact IP+ext+int combination
#
# Blank lines and lines starting with # are ignored.
#
# Examples (remove the leading # to enable):
#   25
#   3389-3390
#   192.168.1.50:8080
#   ip=192.168.1.60
